      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Merrin & Sallow Architects, a fictional practice. First you're on the red team: look at what's public about Catherine Merrin, the managing partner, and her trip this week, then write one short email to her PA, Tomás Carvalho, that looks as if it came from her. Then you swap seats: you're Tomás, with emails and voicemails waiting while Catherine travels. Only Tomás's inbox is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now you're the PA",
        "flipIntro": "This is the email you just sent, as Tomás saw it on his phone. The lines picked out below are what make it believable.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "Tomás read it between setting up two meetings, with more still to do. It fitted Catherine's week and it sounded like her, so he opened the payment form. Being fooled by something built this well is not a sign of carelessness.",
        "flipNoSend": "Your email didn't go out this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, messages like it arrive in Tomás's inbox and voicemail, and you're the one reading them.",
        "flipMissed": "The defence is the same either way: next, messages like it arrive in Tomás's inbox and voicemail, and you're the one reading them.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It got through, but there was nothing in it to pick apart this time, so there's nothing to replay. Next, messages like it arrive in Tomás's inbox and voicemail, and you're the one reading them.",
        "noBlameLine": "There's no mark against you for a miss here. Every one of these was built to look and sound right, and the people who act on them are trying to help in a hurry. A miss here is about the pressure you were under.",
        "defendIntro": "Now you're in Tomás's seat. Five emails and voicemails are waiting. Take them one at a time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if someone senior asks you to pay, buy or send something in a hurry, or to keep it from finance, check before you act, even when it's their voice on a voicemail. Ring them on the number already in your contacts, or ask finance, then report the message the way your organisation asks you to.",
        "defendOnly": "These messages were made by people whose job is to make them look and sound right, and anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "voicePanel": {
          "heading": "A voice is not proof",
          "text": "A voice can be copied. Recordings of someone speaking in public, such as a talk or an interview, can be used to make new sentences in a voice that sounds like theirs. So a familiar voice on a voicemail or a call doesn't tell you who is speaking. What still works is the route: ring the person back on a number you already hold, or check with someone who would know, such as finance."
        }
      },
